    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1fd62c37_76cdd02b",
        "filename": "chip/g/i2cm.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 33,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-10-07T04:00:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "is this not a sequence, this is a bitmap to write into I2C_INST",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 33,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 54
      },
      "revId": "88fb8b8822111cf8f7cc9c337ca5052c4c523391",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1fd62c37_f9ec33f2",
        "filename": "chip/g/i2cm.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 33,
      "author": {
        "id": 1123814
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-10-07T17:01:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes it\u0027s a bitmap that defines a sequence of operations that will take place. In the chip documentation it\u0027s referred to as a compound instruction that is defined by the bits set in the INST register.",
      "parentUuid": "1fd62c37_76cdd02b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 33,
        "startChar": 27,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 54
      },
      "revId": "88fb8b8822111cf8f7cc9c337ca5052c4c523391",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1fd62c37_b69488f0",
        "filename": "chip/g/i2cm.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 56,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-10-07T04:00:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "this is the register address, right?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 56,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 56,
        "endChar": 32
      },
      "revId": "88fb8b8822111cf8f7cc9c337ca5052c4c523391",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1fd62c37_99fbffa4",
        "filename": "chip/g/i2cm.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 56,
      "author": {
        "id": 1123814
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-10-07T17:01:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, this would be the register address. This example assumes that chip_i2c_xfer() is called with out_bytes \u003d 1. The read example and the write example I put here are 2 of the examples listed in the H1 chip spec.",
      "parentUuid": "1fd62c37_b69488f0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 56,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 56,
        "endChar": 32
      },
      "revId": "88fb8b8822111cf8f7cc9c337ca5052c4c523391",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1fd62c37_567c14b9",
        "filename": "chip/g/i2cm.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 64,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-10-07T04:00:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "does it end up with the same bitmask as the one written into I2C_INST?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 64,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 64,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "88fb8b8822111cf8f7cc9c337ca5052c4c523391",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1fd62c37_99d2df22",
        "filename": "chip/g/i2cm.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 64,
      "author": {
        "id": 1123814
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-10-07T17:01:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "b23:0 of INST gets copied to b23:0 of STATUS when the compound instruction is initiated. The difference is that the slave device address bits contained in INST are not copied into STATUS. As the compound instruction (sequence) progresses, the bits 23:0 of STATUS are updated by HW to track the what the current state is. When the sequence is complete these bits should be 0. If there are no errors, then the upper 7 bits will be 0 as well.",
      "parentUuid": "1fd62c37_567c14b9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 64,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 64,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "88fb8b8822111cf8f7cc9c337ca5052c4c523391",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1fd62c37_36a0783d",
        "filename": "chip/g/i2cm.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 87,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-10-07T04:00:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "does this mean max poll time is 25 ms?\n\nShould it be dependent on the clock speed?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 87,
        "startChar": 34,
        "endLine": 87,
        "endChar": 39
      },
      "revId": "88fb8b8822111cf8f7cc9c337ca5052c4c523391",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1fd62c37_b9c8fb6f",
        "filename": "chip/g/i2cm.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 87,
      "author": {
        "id": 1123814
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-10-07T17:01:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes it would be 25 msec and it\u0027s an upper bound that\u0027s enough to cover the longest expected transaction (68 bytes) at 100 kHz. I could modify this to be a lower value for higher speeds, but it\u0027s really only handling the case that the HW doesn\u0027t respond for some reason. If there is any unexpected error (NACK, arbirtration loss, clock stretch timeout) then i2cm will abort the instruction and clear INTB.",
      "parentUuid": "1fd62c37_36a0783d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 87,
        "startChar": 34,
        "endLine": 87,
        "endChar": 39
      },
      "revId": "88fb8b8822111cf8f7cc9c337ca5052c4c523391",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
